So if you know Terraform is actually created by HashiCorp and HashiCorp has open source Terraform. So till the last year, Terraform was actually open source.
But sometime last year, HashiCorp decided to change the license of Terraform. So Terraform is no longer open source, but it is under BSL license.
(Business source license)

What exactly is this licensing change?

Just like how AWS provides EKS Kubernetes as a service, HashiCorp has noticed many companies are providing Terraform as a solution, or many companies are 
building companies on infrastructure and they are using Terraform under the hood. Looking at all this, you know, HashiCorp is not making a lot of money, 
but these companies are making money and they are not contributing it back to Terraform. So HashiCorp has decided to change the license to BSL.
Now according to the BSL license, any such companies cannot use Terraform or cannot sell Terraform as part of their offering. If they have to do so, they need to 
get in touch with HashiCorp support and they have to buy some license from HashiCorp. However, this does not impact the end user.

So let's say in your company, if you are using Terraform, maybe to create infrastructure on AWS or maybe to create your dev environment, staging environment, 
prod environment, it actually does not matter for you because this licensing change is focused on companies that are selling Terraform.
So for you or for your company which is using Terraform to create your internal infrastructure, this licensing change does not make any difference.
So some of these companies which are impacted and some contributors, contributors of Terraform who did not like this licensing change, 
who did not like Terraform from moving from open source to BSL, have forked. A copy of Terraform or have created a fork from the Terraform GitHub repository 
and made this fork open source. And this is called as open door. So technically open tofu and terraform have the same roots, but at a point they are diverged 
because open tofu is actually created from Terraform from the version of Terraform that was open source. Later Terraform moved to BSL.

Terraform or HashiCorp kept adding changes to Terraform. Similarly, open source company or community kept adding changes to open tofu.
Now, interviewer's question is have you heard of open tofu? Do you think it is better than Terraform?

Response: open tofu share their roots because open tofu was actually created from Terraform. It was created from Terraform because of licensing change of 
Terraform in our organization. We just use Terraform to create internal infrastructure. We don't sell Terraform, so we are not impacted by the licensing chain.
So we have decided to go with HashiCorp, Terraform. We have built a lot of HCL files. We are heavily invested in the HashiCorp products such as Terraform vault.
So we have decided, at least for now, we will stick to HashiCorp. And we have been using Terraform only.
However, I personally learned open tofu and I learned that the same Terraform files can actually be used with open tofu as well if given an opportunity.
I'm open to learn open tofu and I'm open to work with open tofu as well.
